\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces $L^2$ norms of the Hamiltonian ($\mathcal  {H}$, ink) and momentum ($\mathcal  {M}$, grey dashed) constraints against time for every run drawn in the main-text figures, each at its highest refinement level. The norms are base-grid averages and compare only within one domain (Sec.~\ref {sec:setup:diagnostics}). (a),(b)~The single throat of Fig.~\ref {fig:single_throat}: level 4 for the unperturbed throat and $\varepsilon =\pm 10^{-2}$ ($+10^{-2}$ stopped, dot), level 3 for $\pm 10^{-3}$ and $\pm 0.1$ (dash style gives the sign, line weight the amplitude; grey, unperturbed; crosses, failures). At $t=0$ the norms carry the discretisation error of the exact data plus the perturbation's own constraint violation, $0.090\,|\varepsilon |$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:model:seeds}). The runs with $|\varepsilon |\le 10^{-2}$ then follow the unperturbed norms until the end states part; $\varepsilon =\pm 0.1$ starts higher and fails by overflow at the compactified origin. (c)~The pure-quadrupole collapse of Fig.~\ref {fig:single_collapse} (gold, its MOTS from $t=33$; dotted, reliable to $t=60$); the late rise comes after that time. (d)~The inflating throat of Fig.~\ref {fig:single_inflation} to $t=218$. (e)~The head-on run of Fig.~\ref {fig:headon_collapse} (rules at the restart times, levels per restart segment). Its level-5 segment fails (a NaN inside the horizon) at $t=38.845$ (cross), with its norm below its value at horizon formation (gold rule, $t=18$). (f)~The $d=6$ merger of Fig.~\ref {fig:spiral_collapse}: the restart segments used in ink, each failed restart in grey up to its failure (levels 6 and 7 dashed; times in Fig.~\ref {fig:spiral_collapse}(d)). (g)~The $p=0.60$ plunge at level 4: the run without a $\chi $ floor (grey) fails by a NaN in the merged core at $t=55.5$ with its norm at its floor, the restart with a $\chi $ floor reaches $t=100$, and a level-5 restart (grey dashed) passes the merger (dotted, reliable to $t=80$). (h)~The $p=0.45$ fly-by: the norms stay at their floor through the pass and grow as the mouths inflate (rule, reliable to $t=67.6$). The short peaks in (g), (h) and the $p=0.25$ run come from the base-grid norm sampling the moving throats' $\chi $ minima: on the $p=0.25$ test pair, four cells next to the minima carry $99.99\%$ of a peak, and neither $\max |K|$ nor the waves show it. At every failure the Hamiltonian norm sits at or below its starting value, so each failure is the interior NaN of Sec.~\ref {sec:spiral:wall}, not a constraint blow-up.}}{21}{figure.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:constraints}{{11}{21}{$L^2$ norms of the Hamiltonian ($\mathcal {H}$, ink) and momentum ($\mathcal {M}$, grey dashed) constraints against time for every run drawn in the main-text figures, each at its highest refinement level. The norms are base-grid averages and compare only within one domain (Sec.~\ref {sec:setup:diagnostics}). (a),(b)~The single throat of Fig.~\ref {fig:single_throat}: level 4 for the unperturbed throat and $\varepsilon =\pm 10^{-2}$ ($+10^{-2}$ stopped, dot), level 3 for $\pm 10^{-3}$ and $\pm 0.1$ (dash style gives the sign, line weight the amplitude; grey, unperturbed; crosses, failures). At $t=0$ the norms carry the discretisation error of the exact data plus the perturbation's own constraint violation, $\clmSeedDefectBox \,|\varepsilon |$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:model:seeds}). The runs with $|\varepsilon |\le 10^{-2}$ then follow the unperturbed norms until the end states part; $\varepsilon =\pm 0.1$ starts higher and fails by overflow at the compactified origin. (c)~The pure-quadrupole collapse of Fig.~\ref {fig:single_collapse} (gold, its MOTS from $t=\clmPureQuadMotsTime $; dotted, reliable to $t=\clmPureQuadTrust $); the late rise comes after that time. (d)~The inflating throat of Fig.~\ref {fig:single_inflation} to $t=\clmFourWall $. (e)~The head-on run of Fig.~\ref {fig:headon_collapse} (rules at the restart times, levels per restart segment). Its level-5 segment fails (a NaN inside the horizon) at $t=\clmHeadonCsmNanTime $ (cross), with its norm below its value at horizon formation (gold rule, $t=\clmHeadonCsmMotsTime $). (f)~The $d=6$ merger of Fig.~\ref {fig:spiral_collapse}: the restart segments used in ink, each failed restart in grey up to its failure (levels 6 and 7 dashed; times in Fig.~\ref {fig:spiral_collapse}(d)). (g)~The $p=\clmEgwPSixty $ plunge at level 4: the run without a $\chi $ floor (grey) fails by a NaN in the merged core at $t=\clmPSixtyExtDeath $ with its norm at its floor, the restart with a $\chi $ floor reaches $t=100$, and a level-5 restart (grey dashed) passes the merger (dotted, reliable to $t=\clmPSixtyTrust $). (h)~The $p=\clmSpiralMomentumFlyby $ fly-by: the norms stay at their floor through the pass and grow as the mouths inflate (rule, reliable to $t=\clmPFortyFiveTrust $). The short peaks in (g), (h) and the $p=\clmSpiralMomentumTwentyFive $ run come from the base-grid norm sampling the moving throats' $\chi $ minima: on the $p=\clmSpiralMomentumTwentyFive $ test pair, four cells next to the minima carry $\clmNormPitShare \%$ of a peak, and neither $\max |K|$ nor the waves show it. At every failure the Hamiltonian norm sits at or below its starting value, so each failure is the interior NaN of Sec.~\ref {sec:spiral:wall}, not a constraint blow-up}{figure.11}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Top: energy, multipoles and flux of the scalar field on the $\Psi _4$ extraction spheres, for the fly-by. (a)~Energy through $R=30$ against time, in units of the gravitational energy that has crossed it by $t=97.6$ (reliable to $t-R=67.6$): gravitational (ink) and scalar with its physical sign (gold, Sec.~\ref {sec:gw:scalar}). The printed ratio $|E_\phi |/E_{\rm  GW}$ falls to $0.56$ over the whole record. (b)~Multipole content: $\ell =1$ carries all but $10^{-6.2}$ of the power; $\ell =0$ and $\ell =2$, forbidden by symmetry, measure numerical symmetry breaking. (c)~$|F_\phi |$ at both spheres, each to $t-R=67.6$. After the blue rule ($t=60$) the inner sphere reads the inflating mouths' near field, which has no counterpart at $R=30$ one light-crossing time later, so that stretch is dashed. The scalar energy leaves as a dipole pulse that arrives before the gravitational burst. Bottom: envelope of the scalar flux $|F_\phi |$ against time, for runs with and without a horizon: a running maximum over $25$ units, smoothed in log amplitude, since the raw flux changes sign every ${\sim }13$ units. (a)~The head-on run (all restart segments joined) at $R=10/14/18$. The common MOTS forms at $t=18$ (dotted). The remaining scalar charge then leaves as one $\ell =1$ pulse, peaking later at each sphere, and decays (dashed, exponential fits). Before the rule the flux, from the open mouths' static scalar charge, is canonically ingoing; only the outgoing part is integrated. (b)~One sphere per end state (one extraction radius each). The merger, behind its horizon from $t=13$, decays like the head-on (dotted fit). The fly-by, without a horizon, shows its complete single pulse (reliable to $t-R=67.6$). Mid-grey: the single throat twice, the $\varepsilon =+10^{-2}$ level-4{} collapse (solid, MOTS from $t=11$) and the unperturbed level-4{} throat (dotted), which inflates without a horizon and grows in the monopole. A horizon switches the scalar flux off exponentially; without one, the flux ends only with the encounter, or grows while a throat inflates.}}{22}{figure.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scalar_channel}{{12}{22}{Top: energy, multipoles and flux of the scalar field on the $\Psi _4$ extraction spheres, for the fly-by. (a)~Energy through $R=\clmGwScalarRadiusOuter $ against time, in units of the gravitational energy that has crossed it by $t=\clmGwScalarGateOuter $ (reliable to $t-R=\clmFlybyTrustRetarded $): gravitational (ink) and scalar with its physical sign (gold, Sec.~\ref {sec:gw:scalar}). The printed ratio $|E_\phi |/E_{\rm GW}$ falls to $\clmGwScalarRatioFull $ over the whole record. (b)~Multipole content: $\ell =1$ carries all but $10^{-\clmGwScalarDipoleShare }$ of the power; $\ell =0$ and $\ell =2$, forbidden by symmetry, measure numerical symmetry breaking. (c)~$|F_\phi |$ at both spheres, each to $t-R=\clmFlybyTrustRetarded $. After the blue rule ($t=\clmGwScalarCut $) the inner sphere reads the inflating mouths' near field, which has no counterpart at $R=\clmGwScalarRadiusOuter $ one light-crossing time later, so that stretch is dashed. The scalar energy leaves as a dipole pulse that arrives before the gravitational burst. Bottom: envelope of the scalar flux $|F_\phi |$ against time, for runs with and without a horizon: a running maximum over $\clmGwEnvWindow $ units, smoothed in log amplitude, since the raw flux changes sign every ${\sim }\clmGwFluxSignStretch $ units. (a)~The head-on run (all restart segments joined) at $R=\clmGwHeadonRadiusA /\clmGwHeadonRadiusB /\clmGwHeadonRadiusC $. The common MOTS forms at $t=\clmHeadonCsmMotsTime $ (dotted). The remaining scalar charge then leaves as one $\ell =1$ pulse, peaking later at each sphere, and decays (dashed, exponential fits). Before the rule the flux, from the open mouths' static scalar charge, is canonically ingoing; only the outgoing part is integrated. (b)~One sphere per end state (one extraction radius each). The merger, behind its horizon from $t=\clmMergerMotsTime $, decays like the head-on (dotted fit). The fly-by, without a horizon, shows its complete single pulse (reliable to $t-R=\clmFlybyTrustRetarded $). Mid-grey: the single throat twice, the $\varepsilon =\clmGwKickedEps $ level-\clmGwKickedLevel {} collapse (solid, MOTS from $t=\clmGwKickedMotsTime $) and the unperturbed level-\clmGwSingleLevel {} throat (dotted), which inflates without a horizon and grows in the monopole. A horizon switches the scalar flux off exponentially; without one, the flux ends only with the encounter, or grows while a throat inflates}{figure.12}{}}
\newlabel{fig:scalar_censorship}{{12}{22}{Top: energy, multipoles and flux of the scalar field on the $\Psi _4$ extraction spheres, for the fly-by. (a)~Energy through $R=\clmGwScalarRadiusOuter $ against time, in units of the gravitational energy that has crossed it by $t=\clmGwScalarGateOuter $ (reliable to $t-R=\clmFlybyTrustRetarded $): gravitational (ink) and scalar with its physical sign (gold, Sec.~\ref {sec:gw:scalar}). The printed ratio $|E_\phi |/E_{\rm GW}$ falls to $\clmGwScalarRatioFull $ over the whole record. (b)~Multipole content: $\ell =1$ carries all but $10^{-\clmGwScalarDipoleShare }$ of the power; $\ell =0$ and $\ell =2$, forbidden by symmetry, measure numerical symmetry breaking. (c)~$|F_\phi |$ at both spheres, each to $t-R=\clmFlybyTrustRetarded $. After the blue rule ($t=\clmGwScalarCut $) the inner sphere reads the inflating mouths' near field, which has no counterpart at $R=\clmGwScalarRadiusOuter $ one light-crossing time later, so that stretch is dashed. The scalar energy leaves as a dipole pulse that arrives before the gravitational burst. Bottom: envelope of the scalar flux $|F_\phi |$ against time, for runs with and without a horizon: a running maximum over $\clmGwEnvWindow $ units, smoothed in log amplitude, since the raw flux changes sign every ${\sim }\clmGwFluxSignStretch $ units. (a)~The head-on run (all restart segments joined) at $R=\clmGwHeadonRadiusA /\clmGwHeadonRadiusB /\clmGwHeadonRadiusC $. The common MOTS forms at $t=\clmHeadonCsmMotsTime $ (dotted). The remaining scalar charge then leaves as one $\ell =1$ pulse, peaking later at each sphere, and decays (dashed, exponential fits). Before the rule the flux, from the open mouths' static scalar charge, is canonically ingoing; only the outgoing part is integrated. (b)~One sphere per end state (one extraction radius each). The merger, behind its horizon from $t=\clmMergerMotsTime $, decays like the head-on (dotted fit). The fly-by, without a horizon, shows its complete single pulse (reliable to $t-R=\clmFlybyTrustRetarded $). Mid-grey: the single throat twice, the $\varepsilon =\clmGwKickedEps $ level-\clmGwKickedLevel {} collapse (solid, MOTS from $t=\clmGwKickedMotsTime $) and the unperturbed level-\clmGwSingleLevel {} throat (dotted), which inflates without a horizon and grows in the monopole. A horizon switches the scalar flux off exponentially; without one, the flux ends only with the encounter, or grows while a throat inflates}{figure.12}{}}
